# sorting.zd - two sorts written in the language, next to the one the
# language provides, all three over the same twenty numbers.
#
# `sort each n by n` is a pipeline clause and it lowers to the platform's
# own sort. That is the right thing for a program to use and the wrong
# thing to learn anything from, so this file writes insertion sort and
# merge sort out longhand, counts the comparisons each one makes, and puts
# the three answers side by side. If they ever disagree, the page says so.
#
# WHAT THE COUNTS ARE FOR. Insertion sort makes 119 comparisons on these
# twenty numbers and merge sort makes 63. That gap is the whole of the
# difference between n squared and n log n, and it is the sort of thing a
# language should be able to show rather than assert. Neither number is
# written into this file: both are computed by the programs, and the test
# that pins them would fail if either sort changed shape.
#
# WHAT THE LANGUAGE MADE HARD.
#
#   1. **No comparator can be passed** (§17.2.5, by design). So `sort each
#      … by` takes a key rather than a two-argument function, and a sort
#      written by hand hard-codes `<` on the element itself. A descending
#      sort of numbers is `reverse of` an ascending one, and a sort by two
#      keys at once is not spellable at all without folding both into a
#      single number.
#
#   2. **The built-in sort is stable, and that is now the language's
#      promise rather than the platform's.** This file is where the
#      question was asked — five racers, two heats, sorted by heat, at
#      the bottom — and #114 is closed because the answer got written
#      down. `zdc-codegen/src/stmt.rs` states the guarantee at the `Sort`
#      lowering and names the two things that carry it: the method is
#      `Array.prototype.sort`, stable by specification since ES2019, and
#      the comparator's last arm is `0`, so equal keys are left where
#      they were. `emission.rs::a_sort_is_stable_so_a_second_sort_keeps_
#      the_first_ones_order` fails if either changes.
#
#      This is what makes item 1 survivable: with no comparators, a sort
#      by two keys has to be two passes, least significant first — and
#      two passes is only correct if the sort is stable. The guarantee is
#      not a nicety here, it is what makes that class of sort expressible
#      at all.
#
#   3. **Merge sort's recursion is not a tail call**, because it has to
#      merge two answers. Its stack depth is therefore logarithmic rather
#      than constant, which is fine: log n is 5 for twenty elements and 20
#      for a million.
#
#   4. **The merge itself very nearly was not one frame either**, and that
#      is the finding this file contributed. The tail-call rewrite fired
#      when a function called itself and not when two functions called
#      each other, so the obvious two-function spelling of a merge ran the
#      host out of stack at 3200 elements.
#
#      That is fixed (#198). A cycle of functions that give the result of
#      calling one another is now emitted as a trampoline, and the
#      two-function spelling merges a hundred thousand exactly as the
#      nested one below does. The note on `merged` has the measurement and
#      the reason the nesting is kept anyway.

# One sort's answer and what it cost to get there.
record Run
    items    is List of Whole
    compares is Whole

state numbers is client List of Whole starting [
    38,
    27,
    43,
    3,
    9,
    82,
    10,
    55,
    1,
    76,
    20,
    4,
    64,
    31,
    91,
    7,
    48,
    12,
    60,
    25]

# Which sort the page is showing its working for. One signal, moved by a
# click, and the three derived views below recompute from it.
state showing is client Whole starting 0

function followedBy with before, after
    give flatten of [before, after]

# --- insertion sort ------------------------------------------------------

# Take one element at a time and put it where it belongs in what is
# already sorted. O(n squared) comparisons and O(n squared) copying, since
# `insertAt` rebuilds the list.
#
# The outer walk is a `fold each` (#33) and the inner one carries its
# answer, so both are one frame.
#
# This is the shape #33 said would be the common one: the accumulator is
# not a number but a `Run`, the sorted list *and* the comparison count
# together, because a fold that answered only one of them would be
# measuring nothing. A record is one expression, so it is one seed and one
# step, and the second top-level function that used to thread it through
# as a parameter is gone.
function insertionSort of items
    from items
    fold each value into run starting (
        Run with items is empty,
        compares is 0) to (insertOne with sorted is run.items,
        value is value,
        place is 0,
        compares is run.compares)

# `value` goes in front of the first element it is smaller than, or on the
# end if there is none. Every element it passes is one comparison.
function insertOne with sorted, value, place, compares
    when listAt with value is sorted, index is place
        None
            give Run with items is (append value to sorted), compares is compares
        Some with here
            if value < here
                give Run with items is (
                    insertAt with items is sorted,
                    index is place,
                    item is value),
                    compares is compares + 1
            give insertOne with sorted is sorted,
                value is value,
                place is place + 1,
                compares is compares + 1

# --- merge sort ----------------------------------------------------------

# Split in half, sort each half, merge. The one function in these examples
# whose recursion is not in tail position: it has two calls to make and
# then work to do with both answers, so the emitter cannot turn either
# into a jump. Depth is log n, which is 5 for twenty elements.
function mergeSort of items
    if (length of items) < 2
        give Run with items is items, compares is 0
    with half is (
        valueOr with maybe is (quotient with value is (length of items), divisor is 2),
        fallback is 0)
    with left is (mergeSort of (listTake with items is items, count is half))
    with right is (mergeSort of (listDrop with items is items, count is half))
    give merged with left is left.items,
        right is right.items,
        a is 0,
        b is 0,
        taken is empty,
        compares is left.compares + right.compares

# Two sorted lists into one, walking both with an index rather than
# consuming with `rest of`, because `rest of` copies and would make a
# linear merge quadratic. `prelude/list.zd` records the measurement.
#
# When either side runs out the other is appended whole, and that costs no
# comparisons, which is why merge sort's count is below n log n rather
# than at it.
#
# WHY THE SECOND `when` IS NESTED AND NOT A SECOND FUNCTION. It needs an
# element from each side, and `when` is a statement, so the other spelling
# is one `when` per side with the second in a helper the first tail-calls.
#
# **That spelling used to run the host out of stack**, and this file is
# where it was found. Written as `merged` and `mergedAgainst`, the merge
# returned for 1600 elements and died with "Maximum call stack size
# exceeded" for 3200, because the emitter turned a call in tail position
# into a jump only when a function called *itself*.
#
# #198 closed that. Both spellings now merge a hundred thousand: measured
# on the two-function form after the fix, 100,000 elements, where 3,200
# used to be fatal. A cycle of mutually tail-calling functions is emitted
# as a trampoline — each body becomes a step that returns a marker instead
# of calling across, and a wrapper drives them in a loop.
#
# The nesting is kept because it is still the better spelling, not because
# it is the only surviving one: one function rather than two, no argument
# threaded through a helper only to be handed back, and the self-call it
# ends in is `continue $tail`, which allocates nothing where the
# trampoline allocates one marker per crossing.
function merged with left, right, a, b, taken, compares
    when listAt with value is left, index is a
        None
            give Run with items is (
                followedBy with before is taken,
                after is (listDrop with items is right, count is b)),
                compares is compares
        Some with x
            when listAt with value is right, index is b
                None
                    give Run with items is (
                        followedBy with before is taken,
                        after is (listDrop with items is left, count is a)),
                        compares is compares
                Some with y
                    if x <= y
                        give merged with left is left,
                            right is right,
                            a is a + 1,
                            b is b,
                            taken is (append x to taken),
                            compares is compares + 1
                    give merged with left is left,
                        right is right,
                        a is a,
                        b is b + 1,
                        taken is (append y to taken),
                        compares is compares + 1

# --- the sort the language provides --------------------------------------

# One clause. It lowers to the platform's sort, which is why it has no
# comparison count to report: the comparisons happen somewhere this
# program cannot see.
function pipelineSort of items
    from items
    sort each item by item

# --- do they agree? ------------------------------------------------------

# Element by element, because `is` on two lists compares references and
# these three lists were built by three different routes.
function sameAs with left, right, index
    if (length of left) is not (length of right)
        give no
    when listAt with value is left, index is index
        None
            give yes
        Some with x
            if (
                valueOr with maybe is (listAt with value is right, index is index),
                fallback is 0 - 1) is not x
                give no
            give sameAs with left is left, right is right, index is index + 1

# --- stability, measured -------------------------------------------------

record Racer
    name is Text
    heat is Whole

state racers is client List of Racer starting [
    (Racer with name is "ada", heat is 2),
    (Racer with name is "bo", heat is 1),
    (Racer with name is "cy", heat is 2),
    (Racer with name is "di", heat is 1),
    (Racer with name is "ed", heat is 2)]

function byHeat of entrants
    from entrants
    sort each racer by racer.heat

function namesOf of entrants
    from entrants
    map each racer to racer.name

# --- what the page shows -------------------------------------------------

state insertion is client Run from insertionSort of numbers
state merge     is client Run from mergeSort of numbers
state builtIn   is client List of Whole from pipelineSort of numbers

state agreeOnMerge   is client Truth from sameAs with left is insertion.items,
    right is merge.items,
    index is 0
state agreeOnBuiltIn is client Truth from sameAs with left is insertion.items,
    right is builtIn,
    index is 0

function shown of ns
    from ns
    map each n to (text of n)

function asRow of ns
    give join with parts is (shown of ns), using is " "

state given    is client Text from asRow of numbers
state answer   is client Text from asRow of insertion.items
state stableAs is client Text from join with parts is (namesOf of (byHeat of racers)),
    using is " "

# The list the working is shown for: how far insertion sort has got after
# `showing` elements have been placed.
state placed  is client Run from insertionUpTo with items is numbers, stop is showing

function insertionUpTo with items, stop
    give insertUntil with items is items,
        index is 0,
        stop is stop,
        run is (Run with items is empty, compares is 0)

function insertUntil with items, index, stop, run
    if index >= stop
        give run
    when listAt with value is items, index is index
        None
            give run
        Some with value
            give insertUntil with items is items,
                index is index + 1,
                stop is stop,
                run is (
                    insertOne with sorted is run.items,
                    value is value,
                    place is 0,
                    compares is run.compares)

state placedRow is client Text from asRow of placed.items

view
    Column
        Heading "Three sorts, one list"
        Row
            Text "given: "
            Text given, color is "grey"
        Row
            Text "sorted: "
            Text answer, weight is "bold"

        Column padding is 8
            Heading "what each one cost"
            Row
                Text "insertion sort, comparisons: "
                Text insertion.compares, weight is "bold"
            Row
                Text "merge sort, comparisons: "
                Text merge.compares, weight is "bold"
            Text "The built-in sort reports nothing, because it is the platform's and the comparisons happen where this program cannot count them.",
                color is "grey"

        Column padding is 8
            Heading "do they agree?"
            Row
                Text "insertion sort and merge sort: "
                if agreeOnMerge
                    Text "the same list", color is "green"
                otherwise
                    Text "they disagree", color is "red"
            Row
                Text "insertion sort and sort each: "
                if agreeOnBuiltIn
                    Text "the same list", color is "green"
                otherwise
                    Text "they disagree", color is "red"

        Column padding is 8
            Heading "insertion sort, one element at a time"
            Row
                Button "place one more"
                    on click
                        add 1 to showing
                Button "start again"
                    on click
                        set showing to 0
            Row
                Text "placed: "
                Text showing
            Row
                Text "sorted so far: "
                Text placedRow, weight is "bold"
            Row
                Text "comparisons so far: "
                Text placed.compares

        Column padding is 8
            Heading "is the built-in sort stable?"
            Text "Five racers in two heats, sorted by heat. Ada, Cy and Ed are all in heat two and were given in that order."
            Row
                Text "after sorting: "
                Text stableAs, weight is "bold"
            Text "That is bo di ada cy ed: ties keep their input order. The language promises it, and a test fails if the emitter stops keeping the promise.",
                color is "grey"
